309:## P15 · property
310:For any case log, read in any window, command or thread and through any CaseLog object or path to the case folder: the holder of the case, sign-off included, and any reader that finds the case free SHALL report any changed, removed or reordered entry as broken and an incomplete last entry as incomplete, naming the entry; while another writer holds the case, a reader SHALL report a change, removal or reordering at or before the recorded entries as broken, naming the entry, judged from a steady read, SHALL report what follows them as being saved, never as intact and never as broken, and SHALL give the recorded entries, and the rest as being saved, only from a steady read through which one writer held the case, which it knows when it held the gate through the read; with the gate busy and the case lock held, it SHALL name only a change, removal or reordering at or before the recorded entries that a steady read shows, and otherwise claim nothing; and with no steady read within its retries, or with every attempt ending in that corner with no such fault, it SHALL claim nothing about any entry. A lock the reader's own thread holds through another CaseLog object or path, and a lock that cannot be taken or tested, are not another holder. A reader's read SHALL count only while the lock files it counts on are still the case's lock files: the case lock it holds and the gate it passed, for the holder of the case, sign-off included; the case lock it holds shared, for a reader that finds the gate busy; and the gate it holds through the read and the case lock behind it, for any other reader. A read across one that was removed or replaced SHALL be taken again, by the holder as a reader that does not hold the case, and nothing SHALL be claimed from it, and with every attempt within the retries read so, nothing SHALL be claimed about any entry. Lock-file interference beyond a read across a lock file the reader counts on is outside what a reader can know; a check made after it stops gives the true answer. For any two case-log writers on one case, whether two processes, two threads sharing one CaseLog object or two CaseLog objects in one process, at most one SHALL hold the case at any moment, and the holder SHALL run one save at a time: a save begun inside another on the same thread SHALL be refused before it writes anything, and a save SHALL be refused when the log grew since its check. Scope: case folders on a local disk; no fork while the case-log lock is held, and a forked child's writes through an inherited CaseLog are refused. Scope (revision 10; the steps named in revision 12; the repair boundary in revision 13; the take-back and its third answer in revision 17; a refusal partway through the line in revision 19; no report of nothing written once the line is in the log in revision 20): the case lock files are the tool's own; removing or replacing either while the case is in use is outside the at-most-one-holder guarantee, as hand-editing the log is. A writer SHALL re-check its lock before each step that changes a file a reader reads (the empty head record written before the first entry's line, each entry's line, and each rename that publishes a head record or a repaired log, a take-back's included; writing a temporary file that nobody reads is not such a step) and SHALL refuse the step if the case lock file was removed or replaced. A writer refused once any part of its line is written, and before its head record is published, SHALL report its save as not recorded, never as nothing written, and the part written, the whole line or less, stays past the recorded entries as a torn entry, as a crash leaves it. Once any part of a save's line is in the log, which for a repair is once its repaired log has been renamed over the log, no part of what the save's writer is told, the words of any refusal or error it carries included, SHALL say that nothing was written, whatever its answer. A save whose read-back fails SHALL take back the head record it published while that head record is still its own, so that its entry stays past the recorded entries and the save is reported as not recorded; a save whose take-back is not made SHALL be reported as not confirmed, neither recorded nor not recorded, and SHALL ask for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved. If such interference lets two writers each be told that a save at the same log position was recorded, the log SHALL be reported broken as R43 says, never intact, and under such interference a log that reads intact SHALL hold the entry of every save whose writer was told it was recorded; a log that lacks only the entry of a save reported as not recorded or not confirmed, which the next holder's repair removed once a person confirmed it, is within the guarantee.

